{"text": "\u201cCHARGE NUMBER: Various\n\nPROGRAM TITLE: Analytical Investigations\n\nWRITTEN BY: M. C. Bourlas, W. R. Harvey, E. T. Oakley and\nD. C. Watson\nDAT February 10, 1978\n\nA. Tobacco/Water System - Studies involving the equiiibration\nof molecular sieve dried E-55 bright usizg H,'90 indicate that the\nreduction in H)!#0 (as determined from the collected vapors of oven\ndried tobacco) is due to both the generation of H2'\u00aeO from decomposi-\ntion and an exchange phenomenon. The latter has been substantiated\nby extracting the equilibrated oven treated tobacco with CHCl: and\nanalyzing the acetic acid for oxygen-18 incorporation. Approximately\n343 of the available acetic acid (+500 ug) was found to contain 1\u00b00.\nWe are continuing to look for other substances which may have been\ninvolved in the exchange process.\n\nB. Moisture Measurement in Tobacco - Thus far, we have\nestablished that the determination of moisture in containerized\ntobacco cannot be made by either a relative humidity probe or an\ninfrared reflectance method. In seeking for alternatives, it was\ndecided that a Forte moisture analyzer at Primary Processing may\nbe able to determine the moisture content of an entire hogshead.\nEfforts are currently being made to coordinate the activities\nbetreen the Leaf Department, the Stemmery and Primary Processing\nto test the moisture device.\n\nydes in Smoke by HPLC - Work on the above determination\ncontinues. It was found that a more concentrated solution of\n2, 4-dinitrophenylhydrazine was necessary for complete trapping of\nihe aldehydes. The 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone derivatives of the\nil\u00e9ehydes are being used as standards because of their greater\n\nstability.\n\nCrotonaldehyde still cannot be resolved from a minor\npeak of the acetaldehyde or from propionaldehyde. Baseline\nvesolution has been achieved for formaldehyde and the major peak\nof acetaldehyde. There is virtual baseline resolution of acrolein\nSha there is no interference from acetone or methyi ethyl ketone.\n\nSmoking runs are in progress in attempt to get values\nfor acetaldehyde, acrolein and formaldehyde. Crotonaldehyde\nwill be determined by the Gas Chromatography Section.\n\n828218002",
